description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/feature-compose.ts, the captured trace execution identifier is always undefined because it is read from the wrong field, and three lines of code now depend on it.

The trace store endpoint has two success returns. Its success-sampled early return nests the identifier as data.execution_id. Its normal return puts the identifier at the TOP level, shaped `{ success: true, execution_id, metrics }`. The capture reads only the nested form, so on every real response it evaluates to undefined, the guard below never fires, the variable that mirrors it stays null, and the report field fed from that variable is null on every compose. Nothing errors, because optional chaining yields undefined rather than throwing. Measured live: eleven rows created by this emission after deployment, every request answered 201 within 23 to 442 milliseconds, and the success line never logged once.

Replace these two consecutive lines:
      const traceBody = (await traceRes.json().catch(() => null)) as { data?: { execution_id?: string } } | null;
      const emittedId = traceBody?.data?.execution_id;

with exactly these two lines:
      const traceBody = (await traceRes.json().catch(() => null)) as { execution_id?: string; data?: { execution_id?: string } } | null;
      const emittedId = traceBody?.execution_id ?? traceBody?.data?.execution_id;

Both lines must change together: widening the read without widening the type will not typecheck. The new type admits the top-level field the endpoint actually returns; the new read prefers it and keeps the nested form as a fallback so the sampled branch still works. The guard beneath, the enclosing catch, and the abort timeout are unchanged.

One contiguous two-line replacement, one file. Change nothing else.

